      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Leukotrienes are potent bronchoconstrictive and proinflammatory mediators in bronchial asthma. In a previous study on 16 subjects with bronchial asthma (two randomized groups) we showed that montelukast (10 mg daily) significantly suppressed allergen provocation with specific allergens (mites and pollen) (p = 0.0005). In a follow-up study we addressed the question as to when montelukast should be discontinued in order to avoid a relevant effect on inhalational bronchial allergen provocation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "At the end of the first study montelukast (10 mg daily) was given again to both groups (2 x 8 asthma patients) for 21 days. After a montelukast-free period of one and three days allergen provocation was repeated in the same manner and the results were compared with the historic baseline values without montelukast."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The provocation dose for allergens was reduced by 39% after a montelukast-free period of one day and by 67% after three days. Only after three days was the difference significant (p = 0.017), at which time the control value was nearly reached again."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "We recommend that montelukast be discontinued at least three days prior to inhalational allergen provocation."
        }
      ]},
